59,3,,status,Asuka,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
60,3,1,talk,Asuka,,Plan A is out of the question. Extending night services would destroy the late-turn pool and the already depleted crew establishment. It is a recipe for failure.,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Takumi,,"Plan B is the only one that actually addresses the journey time. By cutting across the mid-city, we reduce the connection waiting that is killing the passenger experience.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"Plan D is tempting, as it might reduce those exorbitant contractor fees. However, Plan B's infrastructure costs could be covered by that ring-fenced construction balance I found.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Servas,,"Plan D reminds me of the contactless pilot from eight years ago. It was a success then, and it would be a success now. It is a gentle, effective change.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"Plan C is a nightmare. It involves the pre-war shells. We will hit voids, the work will stall, and the delivery dates will be lies. I cannot support it.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"I agree with @Jonathan. Plan C is risky, but Plan A is an operational impossibility. Plan B or D are the only ones that don't break the staff.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Servas, Plan D is just a payment change. It doesn't save a single minute of travel time. Why are we talking about tickets when people are stuck waiting for transfers?","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Takumi, because ""saving minutes"" costs money. Plan B requires construction. If we can fund it via the capital grant, it's a win. If not, it's a debt trap.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Servas,,"@Takumi, the soul of a journey is not just the speed, but the ease of the start. A seamless fare system removes the friction of the city. It is a quiet victory.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Benjamin, Plan B involves roadway consents. My research shows those are already delegated to the authority. It avoids the joint operator deadlock that would plague Plan D.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Asuka,,Plan B seems the most logical. It improves the network without adding more shifts to the exhausted rail crews. It uses a different mode of transport to relieve the pressure.,"{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Takumi,,"Finally, some sense. Plan B attacks the actual problem—the time wasted in the mid-city. It's the only plan that actually helps the passenger get home faster.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,I am satisfied with Plan B. It aligns with the available capital grants and avoids the operating budget cuts. It is the only fiscally responsible choice.,"{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Servas,,"While I love the simplicity of Plan D, I see that my colleagues find a deeper need in Plan B. There is a time for quiet change and a time for bold movement.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,Plan B avoids the pre-war shells and the joint operator committee's bureaucracy. It is the path of least resistance and the lowest risk of unexpected stalling.,"{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
